00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:15.930
We cooperative 31.4 trillion
digits on Google Cloud.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.520
So today's talk is
about-- so we're

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:24.000
going to take a brief
look at the history of Pi.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.810
And then we're going to
explain how we did this.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.100
And then we'll explain the
technical details, y-cruncher,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.040
the program we used, itself.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.700
And later, we have a
section for our future work.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.130
So let's take a
look at the history

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.110
of Pi and the computers.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:50.140
So as Donald E. Knuth, the
author of "the Art of Computer

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.950
Programming," said in his book,
human progress in calculation

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.800
has traditionally been
measured by the number

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.930
of decimal digits of Pi.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:02.710
So humanity, as
collectively, has

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.260
tried to calculate
as many digits of Pi

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.889
as possible since 2000 BC.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:15.670
And since the 1400s, we found
the equation to calculate Pi.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.230
And since then, we were able
to calculate Pi separate

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:24.430
from the geometric features.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:29.530
And then, in 1949, we used the
very first electronic computer,

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.960
ENIAC, to calculate Pi.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.780
So they did 2037 digits.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.630
And ever since then, we
use Pi as the benchmark

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.870
for supercomputers.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:42.910
And the last record was in 2009.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:48.400
And the yellow on-- it's hard to
see, but the record by Bellard

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.910
was the very first record
on a desktop computer.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:59.120
And then the previous record in
2016 was Peter Trueb and Alex.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.210
They did 23 trillion digits.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:05.690
And we did 31
trillion this year.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.600
This is the first world
record done on cloud.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.070
ALEX YEE: So there are two
forms of Pi computation.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.170
And we want to differentiate
between the two.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:19.560
First of all, it is to compute
all the digits from the start

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:20.140
to end.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:21.973
So if you want to compute
a trillion a year,

00:03:21.973 --> 00:03:25.420
you want to compute from 3.14159
all the way to the 1 trillionth

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.350
digit.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:28.980
This is the most commonly
talked about Pi computation.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.437
It is very memory intensive
because of the amount

00:03:32.437 --> 00:03:33.912
of digits involved.

00:03:33.912 --> 00:03:36.120
The current world record is
the one that we just set,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.228
31.4 trillion digits
by me and Emma.

00:03:40.228 --> 00:03:42.020
The other form of
computation is to compute

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.700
a small number of
binary digits of Pi

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.220
at a specific
offset N. This type,

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.250
it uses a negligible
amount of memory usage.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.030
So it's embarrassingly parallel.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.610
And the current record is 10
to the 17th hexadecimal digit

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:00.240
by Joseph Takahasi last year.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.820
This method is also used
to verify the computation

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:03.570
of the first type.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:08.570
Challenges to competing Pi--

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:13.970
so computing many digits of Pi
is extremely memory intensive.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:17.490
At the current record frontier,
we're at 31 trillion digits.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:24.050
It requires hundreds of
terabytes of memory to compute.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:28.810
Communication, memory
access, is the bottleneck.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.320
It's so bad that computational
power doesn't actually

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:32.760
matter anymore.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.670
This latest computation that
we had, 31 trillion digits,

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.730
actually had an
8 to 1 bottleneck

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:43.170
on disk I/O. The sustained CPU
usage averaged about 12.2%.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:48.420
In other words, the
computation, the amount of work

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.310
to do computationally,
is actually negligible

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.500
compared to the disk I/O.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.580
So why is computing so many
digits so memory intensive?

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.630
This is actually
quite complicated.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:00.680
But to oversimplify
things a little bit,

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.522
there's a very low computation
to communication ratio.

00:05:04.522 --> 00:05:06.480
The runtime complexity,
the computational work,

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.040
is O(N*log(N)^3).

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.800
But communication is
actually O(N*log(N)^2).

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.170
So there's only a log N ratio.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.840
By comparison, a lot of
dense linear algebra stuff

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.790
tend to be linear
or even higher.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:20.880
And this is exacerbated
by the memory wall.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.410
So for the past few
decades, as some of you

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:26.250
or whatever you know,
the performance gap

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:31.140
between the CPU and memory has
been increasing exponentially.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.850
And it doesn't just
apply to memory.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.870
It also applies to disk.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:38.710
And so in this age, the
only thing that matters

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:40.355
is the disk I/O efficiency.

00:05:40.355 --> 00:05:41.980
So the graph, the
picture on the right,

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:47.690
is actually a run in the
middle of a sample computation.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:51.853
The bottom row here
is the disk I/O usage.

00:05:51.853 --> 00:05:53.770
And you can see that
it's completely saturated

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.620
at about 3 gigs a second.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:57.230
In the top is the CPU usage.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:58.150
And it's spiky.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.160
It's not sustained
because the whole time

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:06.360
it is waiting on the disk I/O.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.510
So challenges, PC computations--

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:12.040
PCs are not very powerful.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.120
It's just one machine.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.805
They have a limited
amount of memory.

00:06:14.805 --> 00:06:16.680
So you can't really go
much more than, maybe,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.930
a couple of hundred
gigs of memory.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.750
And so the picture on
the top is actually my PC

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:25.238
that I built in 2014,
not that impressive.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.320
So because PCs
aren't very powerful,

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:32.180
you need to run them
for a very long time.

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.410
And for computations
this large, you actually

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:38.060
need to use disk because you
can't fit 100 terabytes of data

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:41.290
onto a PC.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.800
And so the usage of
disk to do computation

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.450
is called out of core computing.

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:47.768
I will get to that later.

00:06:47.768 --> 00:06:50.060
Supercomputers-- they have
a different set of problems.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:51.393
First of all, they're expensive.

00:06:51.393 --> 00:06:52.670
And they have limited access.

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:56.320
Access tends to be limited
to those who are in academia

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.610
or those who work closely
with them in the industry.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:03.697
Secondly, they're more
difficult to program for.

00:07:03.697 --> 00:07:04.780
It's not just one machine.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:05.780
There are many machines.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:09.510
So you have to do with
communication, parallels.

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:12.440
And there's a lot different
topologies for supercomputers.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:14.930
Nevertheless, supercomputers
can actually still run.

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:20.760
But as of this date, there are
no publicly available programs

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:21.510
to actually do it.

00:07:24.853 --> 00:07:26.270
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
And now, we want

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:30.980
to talk about the overview
of our architecture

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:33.420
and calculation.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:37.160
So as we said, this is the
first record now on cloud.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:40.940
We calculated 10
[INAUDIBLE] trillion digits.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:45.890
Pi times 10 powered by 13.

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:49.280
And we spent 121 digits.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:53.920
As we said, we use the
program called y-cruncher.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:58.760
And we use one main
compute node with 96 vCPUs

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:01.030
with 1.4 terabytes of RAM.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:04.570
And I think one of the
most interesting statistics

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:08.680
is we read nine petabytes
of read and 8 petabytes of--

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:14.640
we wrote 8 petabytes of data.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.770
And the overall cost is
approximately $200,000.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:23.130
But we have various
techniques that we

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.600
could have done to save cost.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.550
And also, this is the
first time world records

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.510
were broken on the cloud
service, any cloud service,

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.159
and on SSD.

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:39.960
So all the past records
on PCs used hard drives.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:43.880
And we also use the AVX-512,
the Intel's latest [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:49.680
extensions, which probably
saved the power consumption

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.530
during the calculation.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:56.830
Why would you want to use cloud?

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:59.350
Suddenly, it's easier
to run the program.

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:04.010
You can run off-the-shelf
program without modifying it.

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:06.560
And it takes minutes
on the cluster.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:08.630
we run 25 machines.

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:13.010
And it takes about a minute or
two to start these 25 machines.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.470
It's much faster than
going to a hardware store

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:18.830
and building your own computer.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:22.690
And also, it's more reliable.

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:25.300
Cloud tends to have fewer
hardware periods compared

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:27.190
to your desktop computers.

00:09:27.190 --> 00:09:32.570
And we have mechanisms
to reduce failures.

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:36.560
And we line migrate [INAUDIBLE]
machines from one node

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:40.540
to another in the case of
maintenance, et cetera.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:45.490
And even when there is hardware
failure, what you need to do

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:47.930
is only restart your instances.

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:53.580
So you have shorter
meantime to recover.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:56.130
And operations are easier, too.

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:59.290
When you take backups with
your physical machine,

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.500
you need to copy your
entire hard drives.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.990
But on cloud, you just
need to take snapshots.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:07.870
And everything can
be done remotely.

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:10.730
I moved from Tokyo to
Seattle during the project.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:12.730
But I didn't have to move
the physical machines.

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:15.310
And the calculation was running
in one of the data centers

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:15.930
with Google.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:22.370
As we said, that
we had 25 machines.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:24.670
But one of the machines
is the biggest machines,

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:27.580
which runs y-cruncher.

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:33.100
It has then the latest
Skylake processor

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.580
with 96vCPUs, 1.4
terabytes of RAM,

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:38.990
it ran Windows Server
because y-cruncher

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:40.870
is more optimized for Windows.

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:45.430
And it had 30 terabyte SSD
to store the final results.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:51.010
And we needed 170 terabytes
of disk, 170, so 70 terabytes

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:51.950
of disk.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:58.120
So we added 24 additional
nodes providing a storage

00:10:58.120 --> 00:10:59.500
and bandwidth.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:06.010
And each of them has 16vCPUs
with 60 gigabytes of RAM.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:09.280
And they are just Linux
servers and iSCSI targets.

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:12.040
And we mounted the storage
nodes from the Windows Server.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:19.090
When we were deciding
the main compute node,

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:21.990
there were a couple of factors.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:24.800
First, having more
cores or faster CPUs

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.180
means more computational power.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:32.180
So the calculation would
be done in less time.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:36.680
And more memory means less I/O
because we can buffer and bring

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:40.170
more data out at once.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:45.390
And we had two instance
types with the biggest

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:46.460
amount of memory.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:51.960
One is n1-megamem-96
with 96 CPUs.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:56.790
This is 2-socket Skylake system
with 1.40 terabytes of RAM.

00:11:56.790 --> 00:12:02.490
And the other choice is
n1-ultramem-160 with 160 vCPUs.

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:04.530
This is a 4-socket
Broadwell system

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:07.700
with almost 4 terabytes of RAM.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:11.890
We decide to go with the
n1-megamem-96 because it's

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:15.370
supports AVX-512,
as Alex said, it's

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:18.270
extension of the
Intel architecture.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.590
And it's faster than the
previous generations.

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:24.890
And also, having
fewer sockets mean

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:26.780
there are fewer NUMA nodes.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:31.560
That means there will be
less memory access overhead.

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:36.320
So actually n1-megamem-96 could
be as fast or maybe faster

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:38.990
than the 160 vCPU machine.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:44.730
So we pick the first choice.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:48.690
In order to store hundreds
of terabytes of data,

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:51.690
we needed to have network
attached to storage,

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:54.570
we're using iSCSI.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:59.520
So you can now touch up to 64
terabytes of Persistent Disks

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:01.650
to One Compute Engine.

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.540
But we needed more.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:08.620
So we had to attach these
disks separate from [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:09.120
structure.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.430
So this is done on the
operating system level.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:14.980
So it's not subject
to the limit.

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:17.420
And instead of
using hard drives,

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:21.940
that standard Persistent Disks,
we decided to go with the SSDs.

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:26.220
Because nobody has tried
using SSDs to calculate Pi.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:29.810
They weren't record breaking Pi.

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:34.640
And to connect these
disks, we used y-crunchers

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:36.005
on RAID system.

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:42.200
The first reason is it's
optimized for y-cruncher,

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:44.260
so it's more efficient.

00:13:44.260 --> 00:13:47.830
And the second
reason is when you

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:52.090
take snapshots you don't have to
worry about the synchronization

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:52.960
between disks.

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:55.480
If you set up a single
huge file system,

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:58.580
you may have to worry about
the metadata of the disk.

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:00.820
But when you have
different disks,

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:02.380
you don't have to
worry about it.

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:07.120
So I decided to use the
y-crunchers RAID layer.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:12.360
And I run benchmark with 20,
24, 32 nodes and 24 nodes

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:13.780
seemed to perform the best.

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:16.800
So I pick 24.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:21.190
It achieved 26 Gbps
read and 16 Gbps write.

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:24.640
So it's almost
the network limit.

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:31.990
There are additional tactics we
used to keep the [INAUDIBLE] up

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:33.830
and running.

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:36.250
The first one is
the iSCSI drives

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:38.360
are doing live migrations.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:40.940
And of course,
backups and snapshots

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:44.550
are important for these
long running computations.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:46.470
And we also set up monitoring.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:51.820
The first point is
live migrations.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:54.510
So Google Cloud,
especially Compute Engine,

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:58.960
live migrates virtual machines
from one node to another.

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:01.860
In the case of regular
infrastructure maintenance

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:05.340
and upgrades, network and
power grid maintenance

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:08.660
in the data centers, host
OS and the BIOS upgrades.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:11.550
So when we perform these
kind of maintenances,

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:13.830
we migrate the
[INAUDIBLE] machines

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:16.620
so that we can perform
this maintenance

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:21.160
work without affecting the
guest operating system.

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:24.400
And these consist
of these gray areas.

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:27.160
It's hard to see, but
it's more than half

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:30.160
of the total of live migrations.

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:35.590
And small portion is the
possible hardware failures,

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:39.520
such as memory, CPU, network.

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:42.370
If we see early signs
of hardware failures,

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:47.230
we also migrate [INAUDIBLE]
machines to another machine.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:52.120
And it usually finishes in
10s of microseconds to finish,

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:56.410
to move one node to the
other, but not for y-cruncher

00:15:56.410 --> 00:16:00.370
or this kind of memory
intense workloads.

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:04.320
So this is the diagram
of live migrations.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:08.310
So first we start
to copy the memory

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:10.980
from one node to the new node.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:17.040
And then when we copy most
of the data, we stop the VM

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:22.020
and move state from the
old VM to the new VM,

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:24.010
and then restart the execution.

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:28.650
And if there is any outstanding
data pages on the old node,

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:32.170
we continue to copy the data
and finish the live migration.

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:34.380
So the middle is
the blackout period.

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:38.980
So we completely start VM, but
the other two parts, gray outs,

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:42.960
brown outs, so we
keep moving the VMs,

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:47.510
but we see a work in
another [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:47.510 --> 00:16:50.090
But the problem is
memory bandwidth

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:53.970
is almost always faster
than network bandwidth.

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:55.910
So it's a typical
2 socket machine,

00:16:55.910 --> 00:17:00.200
you have probably 200 gigabytes
per second of memory bandwidth,

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:02.870
that means 1,600 Gbps, yes?

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:08.230
But the user network
is around 100 Gbps.

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:11.710
And y-cruncher could
rewrite 1.4 terabytes of RAM

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:13.650
in less than 15 seconds.

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:16.810
You would never catch
up copying the data.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:23.770
And this diagram is memory
profiling results y-cruncher.

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:27.700
And the other is bandwidth
on the 1 socket machine

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:31.280
is more than 50
gigabytes per second.

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:33.110
So it's still faster
than the network.

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:43.000
During the live
migration we start the VM

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:46.460
and copy the essential
memory regions.

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:50.380
And then we start the VM.

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:56.970
So during the brownout
process, the VMs keep running.

00:17:56.970 --> 00:18:01.980
But it just gets slower,
and in this case,

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:06.990
it took about 15 minutes to
finish the live migrations.

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:12.640
And for the record, the typical
live migration is much faster.

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:16.480
And in fact, 97%
of live migration

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:18.660
don't have the post
copy process at all.

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:22.360
So they copy everything
before they start the VM

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:25.430
or restart the VM.

00:18:25.430 --> 00:18:32.600
And 99.25% of the duration
is 660 milliseconds.

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:34.620
So it's usually much faster.

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:39.830
So this is how y-cruncher is
an extreme workload on cloud.

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:42.600
And the default iSCSI
timeout value was too short.

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:47.000
So during the slow
brownout phase,

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:49.520
these drives actually
timed out and the migration

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:51.390
was disconnected.

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:54.860
So I had to increase the
timeout value to 900 seconds

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:58.760
so that these drives
won't get dismounted.

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:05.780
And backup was also
an important point.

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:08.800
So when you run
y-cruncher it always

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:12.270
automatically saves
checkpoints during calculation.

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:16.380
And you can restart
from these checkpoints

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:20.940
when, for example, the
hardware program crashes.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:24.600
But these checkpoints are
overreaching asynchronously

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:25.840
each time.

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:29.970
So if you crashed
during checkpointing,

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:32.730
or if you take backups
during checkpointings,

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:35.850
these snapshots are not--

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:38.950
these checkpoints are
not necessarily complete.

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:43.980
So backups needs to be
taken outside these windows.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:48.180
And we use Persistent
Disk Snapshots

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:51.860
to store the checkpoint
files in order

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:54.580
to make the process
more consistent.

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:59.640
I run scripts to make
the process automated.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:04.380
And manually run the
script once or twice a week

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:11.820
when the y-cruncher program was
not running for checkpointing.

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:14.310
And backups are only useful--

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:16.380
if you haven't
restored from a backup,

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:18.310
that means you
don't have a backup.

00:20:18.310 --> 00:20:21.650
So I actually
crashed the machines.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:24.090
I stopped the program
and I made sure I

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:27.000
was able to restore from
one of the snapshots.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:29.700
So I practiced some
disaster scenarios

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:35.650
before actually running
the main computation.

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:37.830
And using Persistent
Disk Snapshots

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:41.140
is actually compressed
for backups.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:45.790
It automatically compresses
and stores incremental backups,

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:50.080
so it's very fast when
you have fewer changes.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:55.810
For example, the copies of
the same disk size, each

00:20:55.810 --> 00:20:58.710
of the disks is 10 terabytes.

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:01.300
But one generation
is 1.8 terabytes,

00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:06.850
and the next generation is
only 2.6 or 2 megabytes, also.

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:08.680
And it's really fast.

00:21:08.680 --> 00:21:10.480
It sometimes takes
less than 10 minutes

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:12.360
to copy 10 terabytes of--

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:15.160
200 terabytes, 240
terabytes of disks.

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:17.200
And it's also cost effective.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:21.910
Even if you have 170
terabytes of data

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:27.260
it only costs $4500 a month.

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:29.780
It's much cheaper than the
regular persistent disks.

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:37.040
And as for monitoring, we set
up both Stackdriver and Datadog

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:40.090
monitoring, because
we wanted to explore.

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:45.160
And this is the actual dashboard
during the live creation.

00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:48.700
So we put some CPU
numbers and disk numbers,

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:57.700
and we set alerts to go off
when individual host goes down

00:21:57.700 --> 00:22:02.470
and the average CPU usage of
an hour of the compute node

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:03.920
was less than 1%.

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:06.280
So that means
y-crunchers have crashed

00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:09.550
or there was something wrong
with the operating system.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:12.360
And these alerts
worked really well.

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:14.180
And I share this
dashboard with Alex

00:22:14.180 --> 00:22:22.050
so we could independently
check the progress

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:23.356
of the calculation.

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:29.480
So after the calculation,
in hindsight,

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:31.950
this might seem
like a simple task

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:35.990
of running a program that you
downloaded from the internet

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:37.550
for four months.

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:40.250
But before we began, yes--

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:42.770
before we began, we
didn't know that we

00:22:42.770 --> 00:22:48.050
could calculate this many number
of digits with network drives

00:22:48.050 --> 00:22:49.640
or on cloud.

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:55.430
And we didn't know that a single
VM can run for four months

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:56.600
under heavy load.

00:22:56.600 --> 00:23:02.530
Nobody-- maybe somebody
did, but we didn't know.

00:23:02.530 --> 00:23:06.290
And nobody did
try the y-cruncher

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:09.640
with network strives for
this large calculations.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:12.940
And network drives are
also able to handle

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:14.600
these kind of heavy I/Os.

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:19.030
So these are some
learnings we had.

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:21.970
And now we know it's
possible to run these kinds

00:23:21.970 --> 00:23:24.620
of heavy workloads on cloud.

00:23:24.620 --> 00:23:26.560
And that's great.

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:29.840
You can serve your own programs
with the cloud, not necessarily

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:31.815
Pi.

00:23:31.815 --> 00:23:34.190
ALEXANDER YEE: So now I'm
going to talk about y-cruncher.

00:23:34.190 --> 00:23:37.460
So we've thrown that name around
a gazillion times already.

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:39.590
Y-cruncher is the
program that we

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:41.630
used to compute
this thing, which

00:23:41.630 --> 00:23:45.387
I wrote over the past too long.

00:23:45.387 --> 00:23:45.970
So what is it?

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:48.380
It's a desktop application
for Windows and Linux

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:53.910
running primarily
on x86 and x64.

00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:55.730
It can compute
mathematical constants

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:59.120
to extremely high precision,
billions, trillions of digits.

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:00.530
What can it compute?

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:02.785
Pretty much all the
major constants, Pi,

00:24:02.785 --> 00:24:05.090
e, Golden Ratio,
Euler's Constant.

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:09.120
And the latest version can
actually do custom formulas.

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:12.060
So if you want to compute
the square root of Pi,

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:13.010
sure, have at it.

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:19.030
If you hate Pi and you're a tau
person, yeah, it will do it.

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:21.710
Primary uses.

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:25.830
It's mostly for
benchmarking PCs.

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:30.200
I'm a big part of the computer
building and overclocking

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:30.780
community.

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:32.238
And so I kind of
wrote this program

00:24:32.238 --> 00:24:34.830
both for myself and everyone
else in the community.

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:37.850
And so I have a whole bunch of
machines at home overclocked.

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:41.520
And, yeah, I use my own software
to test my own hardware.

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:43.950
So, yeah, for benchmarking
and for stress-testing,

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:47.930
which is the other half
of enthusiast PC building.

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:53.900
And lastly, of course, is to set
records for all the constants.

00:24:53.900 --> 00:24:57.050
To date, it set the
Pi record six times.

00:24:57.050 --> 00:25:00.440
Plus it holds the
records for pretty much

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:03.852
all the other major constants.

00:25:03.852 --> 00:25:05.560
And so this screenshot
is just a simple--

00:25:05.560 --> 00:25:06.800
I think it's hard to see--

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:09.370
a snapshot of the program
computing 10 billion digits

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:10.780
of Pi on my computer.

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:14.990
So history of y-cruncher.

00:25:14.990 --> 00:25:18.450
This thing goes
back way too far.

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:20.480
I'm not sure I should
admit it, but--

00:25:20.480 --> 00:25:23.570
so 2006 April, APCS
high school class

00:25:23.570 --> 00:25:25.970
of my senior year,
the project was

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:29.990
to write a 4 function
calculator in Java.

00:25:29.990 --> 00:25:32.880
And after that thing finished,
I kept working on it.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:33.380
It was fun.

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:36.860
And so over the summer I added
new features like square roots,

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:39.740
trig functions, constants.

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:42.390
And then I started optimizing
it for performance.

00:25:42.390 --> 00:25:44.683
So I added a JNI C backend.

00:25:44.683 --> 00:25:46.100
At some point I
started to realize

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:48.017
that some of the features
were actually faster

00:25:48.017 --> 00:25:51.500
than commercially available
products like Mathematica.

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:54.950
One of which was the
Euler-Mascheroni Constant.

00:25:54.950 --> 00:25:59.520
And so that year, my
freshman year of college,

00:25:59.520 --> 00:26:03.890
I computed 116 million digits
of the Euler-Mascheroni Constant

00:26:03.890 --> 00:26:06.530
for what I thought was a
world record at the time.

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:07.612
Turns out it wasn't.

00:26:07.612 --> 00:26:10.070
Someone had 2 billion digits,
but they hadn't announced it,

00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:10.850
yet.

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:13.080
And so, yeah, that
was kind of a letdown.

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:15.790
But anyway, school took over.

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:18.820
I didn't do anything
in 2007 because I

00:26:18.820 --> 00:26:20.360
have schoolwork to do.

00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:22.910
But then 2008, while I was in
one of my really boring lecture

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:23.410
classes--

00:26:23.410 --> 00:26:25.195
I'm a terrible
student by the way--

00:26:25.195 --> 00:26:26.570
I wasn't really
paying attention,

00:26:26.570 --> 00:26:31.840
and so I kind of came
across a new algorithm

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:34.710
for performing large
multiplication.

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:37.210
I didn't really believe it at
first, but after some analysis

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:39.640
I realized it actually solved
a long standing problem that

00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:42.160
prevented the existing
algorithms from benefiting

00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:44.380
significantly from parallelism.

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:46.150
And so I still
didn't believe it.

00:26:46.150 --> 00:26:48.460
So I went ahead and
implemented this thing.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:50.770
And I finally realized,
oh my god, this thing

00:26:50.770 --> 00:26:52.010
is actually for real.

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:55.390
So for that reason alone, I
read implemented everything

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:57.910
from scratch since
I'm natively in C,

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:00.580
and I built a really, really
big computer, this time

00:27:00.580 --> 00:27:02.590
to try again for this record.

00:27:02.590 --> 00:27:06.580
And so next year, sure
enough my roommate and I,

00:27:06.580 --> 00:27:09.040
we computed 14.9
billion digits of

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:10.450
the Euler-Mascheroni Constant.

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:13.720
This time it was a world record,
and that's when I officially

00:27:13.720 --> 00:27:14.950
named it y-cruncher.

00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:19.720
Y being the gamma symbol for
the Euler-Mascheroni Constant

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:21.530
to the right.

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:26.330
Since then, later that year,
my roommate and I basically

00:27:26.330 --> 00:27:28.460
swept the constants list.

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:31.140
Basically because of the
multiplication algorithm,

00:27:31.140 --> 00:27:33.880
what we had was
essentially a cheat code

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:37.040
that no one else had in a
multi-player online game.

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:39.290
So it's an unfair
advantage that I exploited

00:27:39.290 --> 00:27:42.770
to the maximum possible
fullest extent,

00:27:42.770 --> 00:27:44.170
being somewhat of a gamer--

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:45.150
I used to be.

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:53.570
So 2010 was when I started
to add out-of-core support.

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:55.840
So I mentioned the word
out-of-core earlier.

00:27:55.840 --> 00:27:58.210
Out-of-core is the ability
to use disk storage

00:27:58.210 --> 00:28:00.920
to do computation when you
don't have enough memory.

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:04.120
And so after that was added,
I went ahead and computed

00:28:04.120 --> 00:28:05.470
500 billion digits of e.

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:07.160
That was a new world record.

00:28:07.160 --> 00:28:08.920
And then later that
year, I joined up

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:12.310
with Shigeru Kondo of Japan,
and he, with his much bigger

00:28:12.310 --> 00:28:16.150
computers, basically swept
the remaining constants

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:20.740
sqrt(2), Golden Ratio, and e, as
well as Pi itself, the big one.

00:28:20.740 --> 00:28:23.880
The only one that
really matters.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:27.203
Since then, I've
since left school.

00:28:27.203 --> 00:28:28.620
I don't have as
much time anymore,

00:28:28.620 --> 00:28:31.200
so it's mostly been
incremental improvements,

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:34.680
minor improvements like
optimizations, enhancements.

00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:37.200
It's been rewritten once.

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:40.710
The program has been used
to set five more Pi records.

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:43.190
As well as most of
the other constants.

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:45.300
And during this time,
I started refocusing

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:47.730
the program towards the
overclocking community.

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:49.440
Adding new features
for stress-testing,

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:51.630
and benchmarking,
and validation.

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:53.520
And so people actually
use the program

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:57.480
for competitive benchmarking--
competitive overclocking.

00:28:57.480 --> 00:28:59.020
What is it like today?

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:02.110
Y-cruncher is half a
million lines of C++,

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:05.680
no longer heavily developed,
because I have a job now.

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:08.982
And I'm doing some research with
cluster and super-computing,

00:29:08.982 --> 00:29:10.690
but realistically
speaking, that probably

00:29:10.690 --> 00:29:13.150
isn't going to happen
given that I don't

00:29:13.150 --> 00:29:15.170
have as much time as I used to.

00:29:15.170 --> 00:29:17.140
So to the right is
the computer that I

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:21.883
built with my roommate
in 2008 to set

00:29:21.883 --> 00:29:22.925
the first set of records.

00:29:29.180 --> 00:29:31.390
So features of y-cruncher.

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:34.410
First of all, it uses mostly
state-of-the-art algorithms.

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:36.900
I'm not actually going
to talk about in detail

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:39.180
of any of these, because
you can easily Google them.

00:29:39.180 --> 00:29:41.870
Because it would take me weeks
to literally go into all this

00:29:41.870 --> 00:29:42.370
stuff.

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:43.912
But I use the Fast
Fourier Transforms

00:29:43.912 --> 00:29:47.160
for large multiplication, binary
splitting for series summation,

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:51.460
and Newton's Method for
division and square root.

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:54.530
The program is parallelized
and it is NUMA-aware.

00:29:54.530 --> 00:29:56.560
So it will be able
to utilize really

00:29:56.560 --> 00:30:01.070
big machines with
hundreds of cores.

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:03.620
On the macro
optimization front, it

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:07.880
can use all the latest
Instruction Set Architecture

00:30:07.880 --> 00:30:12.140
extensions like
SSE, AVX, AVX-512.

00:30:12.140 --> 00:30:16.160
On the memory side, it has
tons of cache and memory

00:30:16.160 --> 00:30:20.050
optimizations to
tackle the memory wall.

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:24.490
And then to get over the memory
limit, as I mentioned earlier,

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:28.090
it has out-of-core computation
to use disk storage

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:29.950
to overcome the memory limit.

00:30:29.950 --> 00:30:33.640
And lastly, which is actually
more important than it sounds,

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:36.250
is fault tolerance.

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:37.880
One is the ability
for the application.

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:40.017
to save its state so
you can resume it later

00:30:40.017 --> 00:30:41.350
in the event of an interruption.

00:30:41.350 --> 00:30:42.760
That's called checkpointing.

00:30:42.760 --> 00:30:44.290
And the other one
is software error

00:30:44.290 --> 00:30:48.118
detection to detect
errors that happened

00:30:48.118 --> 00:30:50.660
during the computation that are
not detected by the hardware.

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:55.580
So implementation
details of y-cruncher.

00:30:55.580 --> 00:30:57.860
So as I said, I've been
working on this thing

00:30:57.860 --> 00:30:59.330
on and off for 10 years now.

00:30:59.330 --> 00:31:01.460
It's half a million
lines of code.

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:03.770
I cannot go into
every portion of it.

00:31:03.770 --> 00:31:05.062
I'll be here for a year.

00:31:05.062 --> 00:31:06.770
So I'm going to focus
on three parts that

00:31:06.770 --> 00:31:09.740
are most likely of interest
to the audience that's here.

00:31:09.740 --> 00:31:11.210
Namely the out-of-core
computation,

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:14.060
how do I utilize
disks for computation?

00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:16.040
How do I do checkpointing?

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:18.821
And how do I do fault tolerance?

00:31:18.821 --> 00:31:20.820
So out-of-core computation.

00:31:20.820 --> 00:31:21.570
Motivation.

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:22.320
Not enough memory.

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:26.203
No PC to date has
160 terabytes of RAM.

00:31:26.203 --> 00:31:27.870
And when they do in
a couple of decades,

00:31:27.870 --> 00:31:30.162
your browser is going to end
up using all of it anyway.

00:31:30.162 --> 00:31:31.210
So that isn't really--

00:31:31.210 --> 00:31:34.290
yeah, that's not going to help.

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:36.660
Conceptually, use
disk as memory.

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:40.260
Each C++ object
is a file on disk.

00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:43.110
And use RAM as just
the cache for disk.

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:46.480
And so all the data actually
logically lives on disk.

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:48.340
And if you want to do
any computation on it,

00:31:48.340 --> 00:31:51.132
you have to manually bring
it into memory, do your work,

00:31:51.132 --> 00:31:52.090
and then write it back.

00:31:52.090 --> 00:31:56.760
In other words, this is
a form of manual paging.

00:31:56.760 --> 00:31:58.545
So what are the
difficulties of this?

00:31:58.545 --> 00:32:00.930
Disk is very slow.

00:32:00.930 --> 00:32:03.800
A disk seek is tens
of milliseconds

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:07.470
a cache miss to memory is on
the order of 100 nanoseconds.

00:32:07.470 --> 00:32:10.710
So there is quite a few
orders of magnitude there.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:14.613
And so you can't just take an
algorithm written for memory,

00:32:14.613 --> 00:32:15.780
and just put it on the disk.

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:16.580
It's not going to--

00:32:16.580 --> 00:32:19.880
you'll be here for
the rest of your life.

00:32:19.880 --> 00:32:23.060
Secondly, because of all
the explicit data transfers,

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:26.917
there's a significant
amount of coding complexity.

00:32:26.917 --> 00:32:28.750
Likewise, there are
separate implementations

00:32:28.750 --> 00:32:30.230
for in-memory and out-of-core.

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:32.600
So much of the code is
actually duplicated twice.

00:32:32.600 --> 00:32:35.090
It's written twice.

00:32:35.090 --> 00:32:37.885
And lastly, the last thing you
need with all its complexity

00:32:37.885 --> 00:32:40.260
is something to get in the
way like the operating system.

00:32:40.260 --> 00:32:41.427
And so you have to fight it.

00:32:41.427 --> 00:32:42.920
I'll get into that.

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:46.970
So this picture here is
Shigeru Kondo's computer,

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:51.780
which he used to set
the record back in 2012.

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:55.650
He had lots of hard
drives, lots of red cables.

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:57.822
This is amazing for a
computer enthusiast like me.

00:33:00.780 --> 00:33:02.380
Fighting the operating system.

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:04.230
2009 was when I started doing--

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:06.420
when I started implementing
the out-of-core stuff.

00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:10.050
On Windows you have to
defeat the OS disk caching.

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:11.790
If you allocate a
large file on disk,

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:15.390
and then you start accessing it
the way that y-cruncher does,

00:33:15.390 --> 00:33:16.543
Windows is going to page--

00:33:16.543 --> 00:33:17.960
it's going to try
so hard to cache

00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:19.990
that it will page
itself out of memory.

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:23.413
And so nothing-- the
whole system just freezes.

00:33:23.413 --> 00:33:24.330
Your mouse won't move.

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:25.290
Your keyboard won't respond.

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:26.207
It's just hangs there.

00:33:26.207 --> 00:33:28.290
And so the only way to
save it-- well not save it

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:31.030
is to hit the reset button.

00:33:31.030 --> 00:33:34.600
And so after fighting
this for a long time,

00:33:34.600 --> 00:33:36.540
the only way to
actually solve this

00:33:36.540 --> 00:33:39.470
is to tell the Windows
to get out of the way.

00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:42.820
And so in other words, do
the buffering yourself.

00:33:42.820 --> 00:33:45.420
Which means I have to
deal with all the sector

00:33:45.420 --> 00:33:49.590
alignment, the read, modify,
write, manage my own buffers.

00:33:49.590 --> 00:33:53.680
Again, adding even more
complexity to the program.

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:56.810
Secondly, is security zeroing.

00:33:56.810 --> 00:33:59.250
So if you allocate a 1
terabyte file on disk,

00:33:59.250 --> 00:34:02.080
and you write to the end of
it, what happens in Windows

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:04.690
is that it will actually zero
the entire file from start

00:34:04.690 --> 00:34:05.660
to finish.

00:34:05.660 --> 00:34:08.949
So what should be an ON,
essentially free operation

00:34:08.949 --> 00:34:12.048
to 1 byte at the end of a
file, ends up taking hours.

00:34:12.048 --> 00:34:13.840
Because you're watching
the hard drive just

00:34:13.840 --> 00:34:17.300
grind through the entire
file to get to the end.

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:20.469
This is, needless to say, not
acceptable from a performance

00:34:20.469 --> 00:34:21.520
standpoint.

00:34:21.520 --> 00:34:24.440
Why does the OS do it?

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:27.620
Because the contents of the disk
are going to contain deleted

00:34:27.620 --> 00:34:29.330
stuff from previous files.

00:34:29.330 --> 00:34:32.540
And so if you have a password
there that's been deleted,

00:34:32.540 --> 00:34:36.620
self-deleted, and then you
allocate a large file on it

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:39.409
without zeroing it, you
cannot access that data.

00:34:39.409 --> 00:34:43.310
And so it's actually a
security vulnerability.

00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:45.409
And so the only way to
bypass that zeroing is

00:34:45.409 --> 00:34:48.630
to obtain privilege elevation.

00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:50.690
But it took me a long
time to figure out

00:34:50.690 --> 00:34:53.610
that I had to do that.

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:55.929
2010 was when I poured
the program to Linux.

00:34:55.929 --> 00:35:02.100
And so I had to fight a
different set of OS issues.

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:06.150
And Linux, the failure cases
were less egregious on Windows.

00:35:06.150 --> 00:35:08.140
At least it wouldn't
hang your system,

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:10.650
and freeze it, and
require a hard reset.

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:13.140
But overall, I was
never able to achieve

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:16.020
the level of performance and
controls I could in Windows.

00:35:16.020 --> 00:35:19.710
For example, on Windows I
bypassed the disk I/O cache

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:20.820
by using a special flag.

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:22.020
And it always respects it.

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:25.680
If it doesn't, it fails
the API call and so I know.

00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:29.700
On Linux, the equivalent
flag is called O_DIRECT.

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:31.890
Sometimes the kernel
will respect it.

00:35:31.890 --> 00:35:33.300
Sometimes it doesn't.

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:35.320
It might pass, but
doesn't really respect it.

00:35:35.320 --> 00:35:39.660
And so in the end, the program
doesn't know, in the end

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:42.280
you get hit with sometimes
a large performance hit.

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:49.460
So the next topic is
application checkpointing.

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:51.670
The ability to save and
resume application state.

00:35:51.670 --> 00:35:52.440
Motivations.

00:35:52.440 --> 00:35:52.940
OK.

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:55.400
This computation took 121 days.

00:35:55.400 --> 00:35:58.880
There is no way this thing
is staying up for 121 days.

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:00.752
Things are going to crash.

00:36:00.752 --> 00:36:02.210
There might be bugs
in the program.

00:36:02.210 --> 00:36:04.220
Power might go out.

00:36:04.220 --> 00:36:06.500
If you forget to
disable Windows updates,

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:08.990
that will screw you over, too.

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:11.380
I know.

00:36:11.380 --> 00:36:13.760
And so there is a
very high probability

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:19.160
that it's not going to run all
the way through in one run.

00:36:19.160 --> 00:36:22.440
And so the difficulties of
saving the state is that one,

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:25.323
there is a very
complex program state.

00:36:25.323 --> 00:36:27.240
Like I said, it's half
a million lines of C++.

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:28.770
There is a long execution stack.

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:30.660
There are recursive
algorithms all over

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:33.690
the place, hundreds
of functions.

00:36:33.690 --> 00:36:36.440
There's no built in mechanism
in C++ to do checkpointing

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:37.780
for you.

00:36:37.780 --> 00:36:39.900
And lastly, there
is a huge data set.

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:43.030
This computation required
160 terabytes of data.

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:45.600
And if checkpointing means
copying that data somewhere

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:48.150
else, and your checkpoints
are five minutes apart,

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:49.770
you're not getting
any work done.

00:36:49.770 --> 00:36:52.690
You're just copying
data the whole time.

00:36:52.690 --> 00:36:55.740
And so the first thing,
the most important thing,

00:36:55.740 --> 00:36:59.420
is to actually get rid of
the overhead checkpointing.

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:03.030
Which is to integrate it with
the out-of-core computation.

00:37:03.030 --> 00:37:07.050
So as I mentioned before,
in out-of-core computation,

00:37:07.050 --> 00:37:10.320
C++ objects are
files on the disk.

00:37:10.320 --> 00:37:13.020
And so checkpointing is
simply renaming the file

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:14.880
and then write protecting it.

00:37:14.880 --> 00:37:17.780
So that's essentially
zero overhead.

00:37:17.780 --> 00:37:20.180
And then secondly, it is
to actually checkpoint only

00:37:20.180 --> 00:37:22.620
when the disk usage
is relatively low.

00:37:22.620 --> 00:37:23.570
So while the program--

00:37:23.570 --> 00:37:25.940
so while the computation
needs 160 terabytes

00:37:25.940 --> 00:37:30.310
of disk, most of the time,
it's actually less than 50.

00:37:30.310 --> 00:37:32.150
But it's just those
occasional spikes

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:34.100
that actually hit those
limits that actually

00:37:34.100 --> 00:37:35.100
cause it to be so high.

00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:39.090
And so if I strategically
checkpoint when it's low,

00:37:39.090 --> 00:37:42.160
then we don't need as
much backup storage

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:45.440
to store the checkpoints.

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:46.100
OK.

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:48.140
Checkpointing in C++.

00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:49.270
How do I do it?

00:37:49.270 --> 00:37:52.250
It's a total hack.

00:37:52.250 --> 00:37:55.660
I don't claim this is the
best way, but it works.

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:58.220
And if it ain't
broken, don't fix it.

00:37:58.220 --> 00:38:02.210
So I explicitly maintain a
copy of the execution stack.

00:38:02.210 --> 00:38:03.770
When I enter the
function, when I

00:38:03.770 --> 00:38:06.740
enter a function that is
running in out-of-core mode,

00:38:06.740 --> 00:38:10.870
I call into the
checkpointing framework

00:38:10.870 --> 00:38:13.990
and it tells it that I'm
entering a new function

00:38:13.990 --> 00:38:15.950
for a new stack frame.

00:38:15.950 --> 00:38:18.800
And so therefore, I have
the state separately

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:23.140
that I can use to
serialize on checkpoints.

00:38:23.140 --> 00:38:29.110
For program flow itself, I abuse
fall-through switch statements.

00:38:29.110 --> 00:38:34.870
Essentially, when the
program is running normally,

00:38:34.870 --> 00:38:37.370
it runs through the
switch statements

00:38:37.370 --> 00:38:38.620
as if the cases weren't there.

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:46.460
And then when you're loading
from a checkpoint, the moment

00:38:46.460 --> 00:38:48.720
you start the function, you
call into the framework,

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:50.690
it tells you what
to jump to and boom.

00:38:50.690 --> 00:38:51.370
I'm here.

00:38:51.370 --> 00:38:54.230
And so it skips everything.

00:38:54.230 --> 00:38:57.260
At each case, I have to
declare all the live variables

00:38:57.260 --> 00:39:00.710
in that stack frame, which is
unfortunately very manual, very

00:39:00.710 --> 00:39:01.320
error prone.

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:03.740
So I spend a lot
of time manually

00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:05.310
testing all this stuff.

00:39:05.310 --> 00:39:08.940
But once I had this sort
of programming idiom set,

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:13.140
it was very easy to extend it to
pretty much the entire program.

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:15.650
So the example here
is the actual code

00:39:15.650 --> 00:39:19.400
from the implementation
of e, slightly modified

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:21.140
for this presentation.

00:39:21.140 --> 00:39:25.142
The comments have been
changed to explain

00:39:25.142 --> 00:39:27.350
the checkpointing as opposed
to the algorithm itself.

00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:33.248
Fault tolerance.

00:39:33.248 --> 00:39:35.290
OK, in long running
computations, as I mentioned,

00:39:35.290 --> 00:39:36.670
things are going to break.

00:39:36.670 --> 00:39:40.240
Checkpointing allows you to
pause, resume, and backup.

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:44.060
But it doesn't
catch silent errors.

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:49.360
So silent hardware
errors, they are a thing.

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:52.760
Y-cruncher has been used to
set the Pi record six times.

00:39:52.760 --> 00:39:55.950
Four of them have had
hardware errors, two of them,

00:39:55.950 --> 00:39:58.860
two of those actually had
silent hardware errors.

00:39:58.860 --> 00:40:01.670
And so silent hardware
errors, it doesn't blue

00:40:01.670 --> 00:40:03.920
screen your computer,
it doesn't crash it,

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:06.310
it doesn't quit the program.

00:40:06.310 --> 00:40:08.660
It just corrupts your data,
and so it's all wrong.

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:11.900
And so if that happens, you'll
get to the end of computation

00:40:11.900 --> 00:40:13.110
and the digits will be wrong.

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:15.805
And so you've wasted everything.

00:40:15.805 --> 00:40:17.430
It can happen for a
number of reasons--

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:22.050
CPU memory, disk controller.

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:26.320
And so the only solution here
is to build error detection

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:27.910
into the software.

00:40:27.910 --> 00:40:32.770
And once you catch some
errors, retry, and then resume

00:40:32.770 --> 00:40:33.880
from the checkpoint.

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:37.090
So here is an example of a
typical divide and conquer

00:40:37.090 --> 00:40:38.620
algorithm.

00:40:38.620 --> 00:40:42.220
If you detect a
fault on this node,

00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:43.570
just redo the whole block.

00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:44.070
No big deal.

00:40:47.890 --> 00:40:50.590
So how do I do software
error detection?

00:40:50.590 --> 00:40:52.175
It's mostly on a
case by case basis.

00:40:52.175 --> 00:40:54.300
There's generally two types
of these, sanity checks

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:56.968
and deterministic checks.

00:40:56.968 --> 00:40:58.260
Sanity checks are low overhead.

00:40:58.260 --> 00:40:59.427
They don't catch all errors.

00:40:59.427 --> 00:41:01.660
Deterministic checks, they
tend to be more expensive,

00:41:01.660 --> 00:41:04.510
but they will catch all
errors with high probability.

00:41:04.510 --> 00:41:05.705
Examples.

00:41:05.705 --> 00:41:07.830
If I want to check to see
if any data is corrupted,

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:10.330
I can hash it before and
after the usage period.

00:41:10.330 --> 00:41:12.300
And if they hash the
same thing, it probably

00:41:12.300 --> 00:41:14.080
hasn't been corrupted.

00:41:14.080 --> 00:41:16.000
That's a deterministic check.

00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:18.010
That's expensive.

00:41:18.010 --> 00:41:20.740
You use two different formulas
to calculate the same constant.

00:41:20.740 --> 00:41:23.240
If the formulas are
completely independent,

00:41:23.240 --> 00:41:26.948
and they share no work,
that will catch all--

00:41:26.948 --> 00:41:29.240
if they don't match then that
means something is wrong.

00:41:29.240 --> 00:41:32.410
So that will actually catch
all errors, both hardware

00:41:32.410 --> 00:41:34.270
and software.

00:41:34.270 --> 00:41:36.400
And then we get to the
more domain specific things

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:38.230
like Newton's
Method convergence.

00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:42.040
Newton's Method, for those of
you who are familiar with it,

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:45.135
involves iterating an
algorithm until it converges.

00:41:45.135 --> 00:41:47.510
Well, if it doesn't converge,
you know something's wrong.

00:41:47.510 --> 00:41:50.600
And so, yeah.

00:41:50.600 --> 00:41:52.920
Modular residuals.

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:55.230
If you're doing integer
arithmetic, addition,

00:41:55.230 --> 00:42:00.650
subtraction, multiplication,
you can actually

00:42:00.650 --> 00:42:04.220
keep a modulus of the large
numbers over a prime number,

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:06.380
and propagate that along
with these large numbers.

00:42:06.380 --> 00:42:07.910
Because modular
arithmetic is closed

00:42:07.910 --> 00:42:09.630
under those three operations.

00:42:09.630 --> 00:42:11.588
So you can do a whole
bunch of large operations

00:42:11.588 --> 00:42:14.360
and then check the
residuals at the end.

00:42:14.360 --> 00:42:20.790
This is deterministic, and
it's relatively inexpensive.

00:42:20.790 --> 00:42:22.658
The last one is the
most obscure of them.

00:42:22.658 --> 00:42:24.450
It's hard for me to
explain, but it is also

00:42:24.450 --> 00:42:26.620
the most important
one in the program.

00:42:26.620 --> 00:42:29.040
It is the check, the coefficient
sizes in a Fast Fourier

00:42:29.040 --> 00:42:30.300
Transform.

00:42:30.300 --> 00:42:32.340
So long story short,
Fast Fourier Transforms

00:42:32.340 --> 00:42:33.990
are used for large
multiplication.

00:42:33.990 --> 00:42:36.270
It's used for a
polynomial multiplication.

00:42:36.270 --> 00:42:38.790
Based on the sizes
of the inputs,

00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:41.790
you can prove how
large the outputs

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:43.350
will be for each coefficient.

00:42:43.350 --> 00:42:45.870
And if anything is bigger than
that, you know it's wrong.

00:42:45.870 --> 00:42:48.750
This one has pretty
much 100% test coverage

00:42:48.750 --> 00:42:53.730
of a large multiplication,
which is pretty much

00:42:53.730 --> 00:42:58.360
probably 90% of the computation
in the Pi computation.

00:42:58.360 --> 00:42:59.830
So what's the scope?

00:42:59.830 --> 00:43:03.430
Error detection catches
90% of the hardware errors

00:43:03.430 --> 00:43:05.750
from empirical testing.

00:43:05.750 --> 00:43:10.160
Which is good, but not something
that you could rely on.

00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:12.340
But on the flip side
of that, it also

00:43:12.340 --> 00:43:15.430
catches software bugs,
because the error detection,

00:43:15.430 --> 00:43:18.310
it doesn't distinguish
between software error bugs.

00:43:18.310 --> 00:43:21.610
So what we have here
is self-testing code

00:43:21.610 --> 00:43:24.420
which allows me to be lazy.

00:43:24.420 --> 00:43:26.220
And so if I'm writing
something that I

00:43:26.220 --> 00:43:28.770
know is going to have
100% coverage by a higher

00:43:28.770 --> 00:43:31.710
level error detection check, I
don't even bother testing it.

00:43:31.710 --> 00:43:37.390
Write it and then let the code
higher up deal with it later.

00:43:37.390 --> 00:43:40.960
And so this is an
example of the program

00:43:40.960 --> 00:43:42.940
failing on an error check.

00:43:47.680 --> 00:43:51.010
So, future work.

00:43:51.010 --> 00:43:53.960
As far as y-cruncher, the
program is in a steady state

00:43:53.960 --> 00:43:54.460
now.

00:43:54.460 --> 00:43:56.543
I don't really have any
major future plans for it.

00:43:56.543 --> 00:43:58.990
It's just a hobby that I
work on every now and then.

00:43:58.990 --> 00:44:02.140
I'll continue to make
incremental improvements.

00:44:02.140 --> 00:44:04.480
And as I mentioned
earlier, there's

00:44:04.480 --> 00:44:07.000
some ongoing research
on scalability.

00:44:07.000 --> 00:44:09.670
How do I make this thing
scale better on NUMA?

00:44:09.670 --> 00:44:12.130
On cluster computing
and super-computing?

00:44:12.130 --> 00:44:18.070
But there's very little
effort going into that.

00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:20.500
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
And for cloud itself,

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:24.240
we will continue to
have better hardware.

00:44:24.240 --> 00:44:26.770
We will be able to
target more digits.

00:44:26.770 --> 00:44:31.620
We just announced instance
with 12 terabytes of RAM.

00:44:31.620 --> 00:44:32.790
That's huge.

00:44:32.790 --> 00:44:36.230
And for other
types of workloads,

00:44:36.230 --> 00:44:39.510
we prove that cloud
is capable of doing

00:44:39.510 --> 00:44:41.940
mathematical large
scale computations.

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:47.620
So we plan to find and solve
more compute intensive use

00:44:47.620 --> 00:44:48.120
cases.

00:44:48.720 --> 00:44:51.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

